'::jsonb, '"A plane is inclined to HP and its true shape is needed. Which method is appropriate?"'::jsonb, '"A road section is 4.5 km and must fit as 90 mm on a drawing. What RF is required?"'::jsonb, '"A plane lamina is inclined to HP but parallel to VP. Which view shows true shape?"'::jsonb, '"A cycloid is constructed from a rolling circle. What motion generates it?"'::jsonb, '"A circular plane is inclined to HP. Why can its top view appear elliptical?"'::jsonb, '"A line is inclined to HP but parallel to VP. Which view gives its true length and inclination to HP?"'::jsonb, '"For a circle, where is the normal at a point located?"'::jsonb, '"A long object must be represented smaller while actual length remains clear. What information is important?"'::jsonb, '"Which principle distinguishes first-angle from third-angle projection?"'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"500 mm"'::jsonb, '"125 mm"'::jsonb, '"250 mm"'::jsonb, '"31.25 mm"'::jsonb, '"1:1 full size"'::jsonb, '"1:2 reducing"'::jsonb, '"2:1 enlarging"'::jsonb, '"1:3 reducing"'::jsonb, '"An isometric drawing cannot show three dimensions"'::jsonb, '"Isometric drawing generally uses true lengths; isometric projection uses foreshortened lengths"'::jsonb, '"Isometric projection always uses perspective convergence"'::jsonb, '"Both must use RF 1:1"'::jsonb, '"It lies on both principal planes"'::jsonb, '"It is parallel to both HP and VP"'::jsonb, '"The line is inclined to both HP and VP"'::jsonb, '"The drawing is necessarily wrong"'::jsonb, '"Conic construction"'::jsonb, '"Thread notation"'::jsonb, '"Isometric projection"'::jsonb, '"Cycloid construction"'::jsonb, '"Below the plan only"'::jsonb, '"Left of the front view"'::jsonb, '"Right of the front view"'::jsonb, '"Above the title block"'::jsonb, '"Take the shorter projection as true length"'::jsonb, '"Replace both projections by an isometric scale"'::jsonb, '"Assume either projection equals true length"'::jsonb, '"Use projection construction/auxiliary views or successive rotations as required"'::jsonb, '"Third"'::jsonb, '"Second"'::jsonb, '"Fourth"'::jsonb, '"First"'::jsonb, '"Right-side view always"'::jsonb, '"Front view"'::jsonb, '"Only an isometric drawing"'::jsonb, '"Top view/plan"'::jsonb, '"Concentric-circle ellipse method only"'::jsonb, '"Isometric scale method"'::jsonb, '"Rectangle method or method of tangents (triangle method)"'::jsonb, '"Involute method"'::jsonb, '"On HP and in front of VP"'::jsonb, '"Below HP and behind VP"'::jsonb, '"Above HP and in front of VP"'::jsonb, '"Above HP and behind VP"'::jsonb, '"Sectional view"'::jsonb, '"Scale bar"'::jsonb, '"Centre line only"'::jsonb, '"Unsectioned isometric view only"'::jsonb, '"Use centre lines as outlines"'::jsonb, '"Use continuous thick object lines for visible edges and dashed lines for hidden edges"'::jsonb, '"Use dimensions for every edge"'::jsonb, '"Remove all line distinctions"'::jsonb, '"They meet only at a focus"'::jsonb, '"Both must be centre lines"'::jsonb, '"They are perpendicular at that point"'::jsonb, '"They are parallel"'::jsonb, '"Concentric-circle ellipse method only"'::jsonb, '"Cycloid rolling method"'::jsonb, '"Third-angle projection"'::jsonb, '"Focus-directrix method"'::jsonb, '"Top view on HP only"'::jsonb, '"Title block"'::jsonb, '"Front elevation on VP"'::jsonb, '"Section hatch"'::jsonb, '"Rectangular hyperbola"'::jsonb, '"Epicycloid"'::jsonb, '"Hypocycloid"'::jsonb, '"Cycloid"'::jsonb, '"The method turns all lines into hidden lines"'::jsonb, '"The circles have unrelated centres"'::jsonb, '"The method only constructs a cycloid"'::jsonb, '"Corresponding radial divisions and projected points locate points on the ellipse"'::jsonb, '"750 mm"'::jsonb, '"15 mm"'::jsonb, '"30 mm"'::jsonb, '"75 mm"'::jsonb, '"Plan below front view, right-side view left"'::jsonb, '"Plan below title block regardless of elevation"'::jsonb, '"All views stacked without alignment"'::jsonb, '"Plan above front view, right-side view to the right"'::jsonb, '"1:20"'::jsonb, '"1:200"'::jsonb, '"1:10"'::jsonb, '"2:1"'::jsonb, '"Use hidden lines only"'::jsonb, '"Use an auxiliary plane/view parallel to the lamina or a change-of-plane method"'::jsonb, '"Set every dimension to 1:1"'::jsonb, '"Replace its boundary with a centre line"'::jsonb, '"1:50,000"'::jsonb, '"5:1"'::jsonb, '"1:500,000"'::jsonb, '"1:5,000"'::jsonb, '"Front view on VP"'::jsonb, '"Only the isometric view"'::jsonb, '"Top view on HP"'::jsonb, '"A side view in every case"'::jsonb, '"A point rotating about a fixed centre only"'::jsonb, '"A point on the circle rolling without slipping along a straight line"'::jsonb, '"A cone cut by a plane"'::jsonb, '"A line parallel to the directrix"'::jsonb, '"All circles always draw as ellipses"'::jsonb, '"Projection foreshortens dimensions when the circle is inclined to the projection plane"'::jsonb, '"The real circle changes shape"'::jsonb, '"The scale ratio changes non-uniformly in every view"'::jsonb, '"Title-block view"'::jsonb, '"Top view only"'::jsonb, '"Front elevation"'::jsonb, '"Section hatch"'::jsonb, '"Parallel to every diameter"'::jsonb, '"Along the tangent through the point"'::jsonb, '"On the dimension line"'::jsonb, '"Along the radius through the point of contact"'::jsonb, '"Isometric view without measurements"'::jsonb, '"Omit dimensions because a scale exists"'::jsonb, '"Only line colour"'::jsonb, '"Appropriate RF, clear dimensioning and scale notation"'::jsonb, '"Relative placement of object, observer and projection plane"'::jsonb, '"Number of dimensions shown"'::jsonb, '"Whether colour is used"'::jsonb, '"Number of line types"'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY['0'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- ENGINEERING DRAWING | ESE | medium | TestId 4600913511010129 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4600913511010129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply drawing instruments, bis conventions, line types and layout in a practical situation such as preparing a standards-based engineering sheet before drawing a component. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply representative fraction and selection of drawing scales in a practical situation such as choosing a scale to fit a large object on a small sheet. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply dimensioning and annotation of engineering drawings in a practical situation such as making a component drawing usable by a workshop without verbal explanation. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply orthographic projection and first-angle versus third-angle methods in a practical situation such as producing front, top and side views from a pictorial object. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nA drawing sheet should have a suitable size, border, title block and layout, with space allocated for views and notes. Instruments such as drawing boards, T-squares or parallel rules, set squares, compass, divider and scale support accurate construction. Standard line conventions distinguish visible outlines, hidden edges, centre lines, dimensions and construction work. Use lettering and dimensioning consistently and choose a suitable scale when the object will not fit at full size. Standards make drawings legible and interpretable by others, rather than relying on informal assumptions.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nRepresentative fraction (RF) is drawing length divided by actual length after both are expressed in the same units. A full-size scale has RF 1:1; a reducing scale has RF below one, and an enlarging scale has RF greater than one. For example, RF 1:5 means a 50 mm actual length is shown as 10 mm on the drawing. Select a scale that preserves legibility and available sheet space, state it clearly, and apply it consistently to dimensions and construction. The written dimension normally states actual size, not the measured paper length.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nDimensioning communicates the size and position of features. Use clear extension and dimension lines, legible figures and appropriate symbols, and avoid repeating dimensions or creating ambiguity. Choose line types according to their purpose: visible outlines, hidden details, centre lines and dimension lines should be distinguishable. Annotations should be placed near the relevant feature without clutter. A drawing should contain enough dimensions to manufacture or inspect the part, but not contradictory or redundant values. Apply the relevant standard conventions and check units and overall dimensions.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nOrthographic projection represents an object through views perpendicular to principal planes. The front view communicates height and width, the top view width and depth, and a side view height and depth. In first-angle projection, the object is between the observer and projection plane; in third-angle projection, the projection plane is between observer and object. Therefore the arrangement of views differs. Identify the projection method by its symbol or stated convention, align corresponding dimensions across views, and check that the set of views describes one consistent object.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

