VALUES (460091312102015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical task in Mathematics-II involves this requirement: use a square-matrix scalar to check whether an inverse exists. Explain how Algebra of Matrices applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Mathematics-II involves this requirement: identify the specific purpose or behaviour of System of Linear Equation in Equations. Explain how System of Linear Equation applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Mathematics-II involves this requirement: represent values in rows and columns and respect dimensions during matrix operations. Explain how Determinant of Matrix applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical task in Mathematics-II involves this requirement: represent values in rows and columns and respect dimensions during matrix operations. Explain how Inverse of Matrix up to 3×3 matrix applies, outline the main steps and justify the result with an example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** A determinant is a scalar associated with a square matrix; a non-zero determinant indicates invertibility.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Determinant of Matrix; Inverse of Matrix up to 3×3 matrix; Solution of Simultaneous Equation (up to 3 variable); Determinants and Matrices. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-II problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** System of Linear Equation is covered in “Equations”. A good answer should define its purpose, explain its main components or steps, connect it to Solution of Quadratic Equation, Solution of Cubic Equation, Equations, provide a practical example and state how the result can be checked.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Solution of Quadratic Equation; Solution of Cubic Equation; Equations. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-II problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** A matrix is a rectangular array. Matrix operations require compatible dimensions; square matrices may have determinants and inverses.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Algebra of Matrices; Inverse of Matrix up to 3×3 matrix; Solution of Simultaneous Equation (up to 3 variable); Determinants and Matrices. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-II problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning/purpose (2 marks):** A matrix is a rectangular array. Matrix operations require compatible dimensions; square matrices may have determinants and inverses.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and relate it to syllabus areas such as Algebra of Matrices; Determinant of Matrix; Solution of Simultaneous Equation (up to 3 variable); Determinants and Matrices. State relevant components, formulas, assumptions or procedures.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-II problem; identify the objective, selected method and expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the outcome against the objective and mention a meaningful limitation, error source or trade-off.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

